int WarpVariant(bool depth) { return depth ? 1 | (NearEnv() << 1) : 0; }
// (gpu34) GXV_HRW_WARP_PROJ=0|1 (default 0): the warp without depth (variant 0: zn is one number for the whole picture) with the source's homogeneous coordinate
// q = r + u_z * zn made per VERTEX (it is affine over the quad, exactly as v_tex1 is) and read with texture2DProj: the GPU's texture iterator divides and fetches,
// the fragment program is one non-dependent read, as a copy quad's, instead of a vec3 multiply-add, a division, a clamp, a dependent read and the tint's mix per pixel.
// Same source texel: the clamp to the half-texel rectangle is the texture's own CLAMP_TO_EDGE (every scene / snapshot texture is clamped), which gives the same edge
// colour under linear filtering. Used only where that holds: no margins (the picture is the whole texture), no mirror edge, no tint. Its program is linked where
// variant 0's is; if it does not link, variant 0 is drawn as before.
bool ProjEnv() { static const bool on = EnvInt("GXV_HRW_WARP_PROJ", 0) != 0; return on; }
const char* const kVsWarpProj =
    "attribute vec4 a_pos;\nuniform vec4 u_a;\nuniform vec4 u_b;\nuniform vec4 u_c;\nuniform vec4 u_z;\nuniform vec4 u_p;\n"
    "varying vec3 v_q;\n"
    "void main() {\n"
    "  vec4 p = vec4(a_pos.x, a_pos.y, 0.0, 1.0);\n"
    "  v_q = vec3(dot(u_a, p), dot(u_b, p), dot(u_c, p)) + u_z.xyz * u_p.w;\n"
    "  gl_Position = vec4(a_pos.xy, 0.0, 1.0);\n}\n";
const char* const kFsWarpProj =
    "precision highp float;\nuniform sampler2D u_src;\nvarying vec3 v_q;\n"
    "void main() {\n  gl_FragColor = vec4(texture2DProj(u_src, v_q).rgb, 1.0);\n}\n";
// ---- warp36 (14 Nov 2026; port/hrw36.h has the reasoning and the arithmetic, tests/hrw36_test.cpp the proof on a host). All default 0: nothing changes until a key is set.
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE=0|1|2 (default 0). The owner's "the path I'm on gets blurry and I see double" (Bowser's level, running): depth mode 4 re-projects the scene by the
// camera's rotation only, and while he runs the camera moves 11-18 units a pair (console 2892b; the mode's own comment assumed "a few"): the ground at his distance
// (1200-1500 units) is then left 5-10 px from where it belongs in every second picture, a surface 300 units away 28 px, while the far scenery is right: a 30 Hz picture
// of the near ground inside the 60 Hz one. 1: the scene is re-projected as the GROUND PLANE under the player (the point his shadow falls on, the plane across his
// gravity) wherever a pixel's ray meets that plane within GXV_HRW36_PLANE_R units of him, fading to "at infinity" by twice that distance; a pixel above the plane's
// horizon is at infinity as before. What stands on the ground (coins, enemies, walls) is seen in front of the plane, so it is moved a little too little, never too
// far. 2: as 1 with the normal of the polygon under him instead of his gravity, when the two are within 37 degrees (a slope).
// Cost: no depth snapshot and nothing a pixel: a plane's inverse depth is linear over the screen, so the vertex shader of a 32 x 18 grid (1222 vertices, one strip,
// a static 10 KB buffer) computes each vertex's source and the pixel program is GXV_HRW_WARP_PROJ's own single projective read. One program more (its pixel half is
// the projective warp's text): linked at the first stage load of a run behind the loading screen (StageApplied), else at the first pair; the shader pack keeps it.
// GXV_HRW36_HOLD_TURNS=<turns> (default 45 = 1.5 s; 0 = off): on a turn without ground under him (a jump over a gap, a fall) the last ground point is kept for that
// many turns (port::hrw36::HoldGround, GameScene.cpp port_hrw_player36): "held through the air" in the line.
// Not used (the pair is warped as before, counted in the "hrw: warp36" line): no ground under him (a long fall, flying), the camera under GXV_HRW36_PLANE_MIN_H
// (default 60) units above the plane, the HUD not drawn as a layer (its copy would move with the ground), overscan margins or the mirror edge, the red debug tint.
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE_MAX_PX (default 40): no pixel is moved further than this against the rotation-only warp (the plane's near end is cut; counted when that reaches
// the ground under him).
// plane37: GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE (below, at kVsWarpGate) keeps the plane off what the first picture's depth says is far behind it (the void past a platform's edge).
int PlaneEnv() { static const int v = static_cast<int>(std::clamp<long>(EnvInt("GXV_HRW36_PLANE", 0), 0, 2)); return v; }
// GXV_HRW36_PAR_MAX_PX=<px> (default 0 = never): a pair whose ground parallax at the player (how far the rotation-only warp leaves the ground under him from where it
// belongs; the "hrw: warp36" line has its mean, max and bins whatever the keys say) is above this shows its first picture again: for those pairs a plain 30, which is
// steadier to look at than a 60 whose near ground is a 30. Resumes under 75 % of the limit (the edge rule's hysteresis). With GXV_HRW36_PLANE the ground itself is
// right, so use it there only as a far limit (24 or more).
double ParMaxEnv() { static const double v = static_cast<double>(std::clamp<long>(EnvInt("GXV_HRW36_PAR_MAX_PX", 0), 0, 1000)); return v; }
// GXV_HRW36_BOX=0|1 (default 0). The owner's "bubble" at Mario's feet when he jumps (the Observatory): the player's box (GXV_HRW_WARP_BOX_R = 130 units + 16 px round
// his CENTRE: 200 x 200 px there) is drawn again on the plane that moves with him; the ground round his feet and his shadow are carried along: 10-16 px up and a few
// per cent larger on the first turns of a jump, in every second picture, inside a 24 px soft edge. 1: the box follows his body: from GXV_HRW36_BOX_FOOT_PX (default
// 8) px under his feet to above his head (his centre + 1.6 x feet-to-centre), GXV_HRW36_BOX_HALF_W (default 85) units + 8 px to either side, whichever way he stands
// on the screen; its edge fades over GXV_HRW36_BOX_FEATHER (default 10) px. About 17000 px instead of 40000 in the Observatory and no ground under his feet in it.
// What it gives up: a hand or a foot further out than the box (a spin, a long jump's pose) is moved with the scene instead of with him for that picture.
bool BoxEnv() { static const bool on = EnvInt("GXV_HRW36_BOX", 0) != 0; return on; }
// The plane warp's vertex shader: port::hrw36::EvalK, line for line. u_d = the plane's inverse depth (x, y, 1) and its limit, u_e = the pixel's ray, u_f = the
// player's ground point, u_k = the fade, u_p.w = the zn of infinity, u_p.y = f; the rest as kVsWarpProj.
const char* const kVsWarpPlane =
    "attribute vec4 a_pos;\nuniform vec4 u_a;\nuniform vec4 u_b;\nuniform vec4 u_c;\nuniform vec4 u_z;\nuniform vec4 u_p;\nuniform vec4 u_d;\nuniform vec4 u_e;\nuniform vec4 u_f;\nuniform vec4 u_k;\n"
    "varying vec3 v_q;\n"
    "void main() {\n"
    "  vec4 p = vec4(a_pos.x, a_pos.y, 0.0, 1.0);\n"
    "  float k = clamp(dot(u_d.xyz, vec3(a_pos.x, a_pos.y, 1.0)), 0.0, u_d.w);\n"
    "  vec3 v = vec3(a_pos.xy * u_e.xy + u_e.zw, -1.0) - u_f.xyz * k;\n"
    "  float w = clamp(u_k.x - u_k.y * length(v) / max(k, 0.000000001), 0.0, 1.0);\n"
    "  float zn = u_p.w + u_p.y * (k * w);\n"
    "  v_q = vec3(dot(u_a, p), dot(u_b, p), dot(u_c, p)) + u_z.xyz * zn;\n"
    "  gl_Position = vec4(a_pos.xy, 0.0, 1.0);\n}\n";
// ---- plane37 (15 Nov 2026; port/hrw36.h "Gate" has the arithmetic, tests/hrw36_test.cpp TestDepthGate the proof). GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE=0|1|2 (default 0), with
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE 1 or 2. Console flip-books of c174, Honeyhive: the plane took the ground's 30 Hz jump from 3.1 to 0.85 px (half size), but the stars over the void
// past the honey platform's edge, still without it, moved 1.7 px in every second picture and back: their rays meet the EXTENDED plane within its radius. With the
// gate a pixel takes the plane only where the FIRST picture's own depth, read at the source the plane predicts, is no farther than about the plane's distance
// (in full up to 1.2 times it, not at all from GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE_PCT / 100 = 1.5 times it: the void, the sky, a platform far below); elsewhere it is at infinity,
// as without the plane. What is nearer than the plane (the player, coins, walls) keeps it.
// Where the depth comes from: NOT a snapshot. The first picture's depth is still in the EFB's depth memory while the second picture is drawn: a second picture's
// scene never stores depth (SceneDepth: g_hrwBPicture) and the fresh player's draws already test against exactly that memory (GXV_HRW_WARP_FRESH, on in
// Performance, which is also why the first picture's last scene stores it: WantsDepth). The warp reads it as a texture (FxDepthTexture, the snapshot's and the edge
// pass's way) on unit 1: no pass, no scene, no copy more. With GXV_HRW_WARP_FRESH=0 the gate itself asks for that store (one depth store a pair).
// Pixel cost against the plane's single projective read: form 1 = three NON-dependent reads (the depth at the plane's source, the picture at the infinity source
// and at the plane's source: all three coordinates come straight from the grid's vertices) and a mix; form 2 = the depth read and ONE picture read at the mixed
// coordinate (a dependent read: the old variant 0's cost class, which GXV_HRW_WARP_PROJ was made to leave). Both draw the same picture but for the gate's 20 %
// transition (form 1 cross-fades the two sources there, form 2 reads between them); which is cheaper on the console's GPU is the A/B to make.
// Not gated (the pair is drawn with the plain plane, counted in the "hrw: plane37" line): the first picture's display copy cleared the depth (its memory may
// hold the cleared plane by then), the gate's program did not build, no depth texture at the draw (no DEPTH_SAMPLE library; depth rows wider than the picture).
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE_DEBUG=1: the pixels the gate sends to infinity are tinted red by half (a flip-book then shows the mask the console's depth really gives).
int PlaneGateEnv() { static const int v = static_cast<int>(std::clamp<long>(EnvInt("GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE", 0), 0, 2)); return v; }
// ---- plane38 (15 Nov 2026). Console c175, Honeyhive, GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE=1 and =2: the ground band 3.09 / 3.08 px (= the plane off), the void 0.01 / 0.00 px, with
// "drawn with it 150 of 150 plane draws, no depth texture 0, copy cleared the depth 0": the depth texture was bound and EVERY sample said "far". What the EFB's depth
// texture gives while the second picture is drawn is therefore not the first picture's depth on the console (not stored after all, not readable inside the EFB's own
// scene, or another convention than the snapshot's: the probe and DEBUG=2 below say which). The console IS known to read it right at the first picture's display
// copy, from another target's scene (depth modes 1 / 3, c127 "works on console"; GXV_FXHALF's depth copy).
// plane38b, the cause (console c177: the probe read the far end, code 248 = 788218 of 800158 units, at every point from the mask AND from the EFB at the second
// picture's draw; the SRC=0 DEBUG=2 flip-book was red 1.0, weight 0 everywhere): the GAME clears the depth itself. GameScene::draw3D ends with
// MR::clearZBuffer() (a covering quad, depth ALWAYS + write) before the 2D is drawn, so from there to the next picture the EFB's depth is the far end in every
// pixel: at the display copy and while the second picture is drawn there is no scene depth left to read. The reads and the gate's arithmetic were right
// (zn = 2 d - 1 = f / dist - e: e -1.00025, f -200.025 give -1 at 100 units and +1 at 800158); what they read was the cleared plane.
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE_SRC=0|1 (default 1): where the gate's depth comes from. 1: a quarter-size MASK in a texture of its own (240 x 136; red =
// port::hrw36::DepthCode, the log of the distance over 32 .. 1M units in 8 bits: 0.059 octave = 4 % a step, against the gate's 1.2 / 1.5 limits; nearest of
// four taps), made INSIDE the first picture, before the game's clear: PlanFrame finds the whole-depth overwrite that ends the picture's largest run of
// depth-writing draws and puts the mask before it, or before a copy that leaves the EFB between that run's last depth-writing draw and the clear (the EFB's
// scene ends there anyway: only the mask's own small scene is added); it marks a depth read there, so the scene before stores its depth. Placed at the
// clear itself, the EFB's scene is cut once more (its colour and depth stored and loaded again). A picture without such a clear: the mask is made at the
// display copy. No place found, or the mask not made: the pair is drawn with the plain plane (counted). 0: c175's read at the second picture's draw (the
// cleared plane in this game: everything at infinity; kept for the diagnosis). keepdepth31: with GXV_HRW_KEEPDEPTH=1 (default) the clear is left out on
// a pair's first picture when nothing after it reads depth, so SRC=0 reads the first picture's own 3D depth there (the "hrw: keepdepth31" line counts the pairs).
// GXV_HRW36_PLANE_GATE_DEBUG=0|1|2: 1 = what the gate sends to infinity is tinted red by half; 2 = the second picture IS the gate's reading (a flip-book of B's):
// red = the sample as read (SRC 0: the window depth, SRC 1: the mask's code), green = -log2(1 - sample) / 20 (SRC 0: 0.2-0.3 for ground a few hundred to a few
